Emerging quantum sensors are increasingly envisioned as components of hybrid quantum-classical high-performance computing, enabling new capabilities in scientific, cyber-physical, and machine-learning pipelines. However, their practical utility is limited by environmental decoherence, which degrades sensing reliability. We present SpinTune, a reinforcement learning-based approach that autonomously discovers adaptive, piecewise dynamical decoupling (DD) sequences to improve sensor reliability in the presence of noise. We show that SpinTune significantly outperforms standard DD sequences in preserving coherence by over 80% and derive a metric connecting coherence to AC magnetometry sensitivity. A case study of a real system confirms these gains, positioning SpinTune as a scalable control layer for reliable quantum sensing.